  "question": {
    "text": "The spectral emissive power <math> <mrow> <msub> <mtext>E</mtext> <mi>λ</mi> </msub> </mrow> </math>&nbsp;for a body at temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is plotted against the wavelength (see figure) and area under the curve is found to be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>. At a different temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> the area is found to be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn><mi>A</mi></math>. Then <math> <mrow> <msub> <mi>λ</mi> <mn>1</mn> </msub> <mo>/</mo> <msub> <mi>λ</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msub> <mo>=</mo> </mrow> </math><br />\r<br />\r<br />\r&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\qt0110_fe3f18ee0a0b.jpg\" style=\"width: 171px; height: 144px;\" />"
  },
